## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to wire config-driven per-tenant controls in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, with TLS edge and simple runtime enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for runtime limits
- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket)
- TLS via Traefik edge proxy
- Basic cross-DC deployment considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: token bucket per tenant with 60 rpm
const limits = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/etc/config/tenant-rate-limits.json','utf8'));
const buckets = new Map();
function allow(tenant){
  const now = Date.now();
  let b = buckets.get(tenant);
  if(!b){ b = {tokens: limits[tenant]||0, last: now}; buckets.set(tenant,b); }
  // simplistic: refill 1 token per second for 60s window
  const elapsed = (now - b.last)/1000; b.last = now; b.tokens = Math.min((limits[tenant]||0), b.tokens + elapsed);
  if(b.tokens >= 1){ b.tokens--; return true; }
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely refresh Docker Config without restarting all containers?
- What edge cases arise with clock skew or config changes?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical skills in dynamic per-tenant API version routing, assessing candidates' knowledge of Redis-backed routing state, Traefik dynamic configuration, and Docker-based edge proxies across distributed infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant version routing
- Redis-backed configuration store (tenant_id → api_version)
- Traefik dynamic configuration via Docker provider
- Sidecar pattern for pushing configuration updates
- Multi-datacenter consistency and TLS edge handling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Sidecar that watches Redis and updates Traefik rules
async function syncRoutes(redis, docker) {
  const routes = await redis.hgetall('tenant:versions');
  for (const [tenantId, version] of Object.entries(routes)) {
    await updateTraefikRule(docker, tenantId, version);
  }
}
```
